00:23:06,180 --> 00:23:08,420
It was great. And I'm talking about a really trivial use case.

467
00:23:08,420 --> 00:23:13,220
But remittance just in and out of Africa is double digit a million on an annual basis.

468
00:23:13,220 --> 00:23:16,860
And so if you can capture even a slice of this market, that's big money.

469
00:23:17,220 --> 00:23:20,740
We've done cross border remittance of the traditional sons in and out of Africa.

470
00:23:20,740 --> 00:23:23,460
It takes seven days sometimes through the banking network.

471
00:23:23,460 --> 00:23:25,300
And the spreads are ridiculous.

472
00:23:25,300 --> 00:23:27,780
And if you're doing this on stable coins, you have no spreads.

473
00:23:28,020 --> 00:23:30,420
It's cents on the dollar for gas and then you're done.

474
00:23:30,740 --> 00:23:33,260
So I'm really excited about the MiniPay standalone app.

475
00:23:33,540 --> 00:23:37,460
I would imagine a lot of the remittance piece gets captured in there.

476
00:23:37,860 --> 00:23:39,620
I was going to touch on opera.

477
00:23:39,620 --> 00:23:43,500
So you brought it up in timely fashion with your business background.

478
00:23:43,700 --> 00:23:50,300
Do you think adoption will be more so through an opera or a finance

479
00:23:50,460 --> 00:23:55,940
or yellow card product that to some degree are accounts that are a little bit more free form

480
00:23:55,940 --> 00:23:58,940
where they give you access and it's a little bit of do what you like with it

481
00:23:59,180 --> 00:24:04,220
or more of a transfer wise Felix type experience where it's a purpose

482
00:24:04,220 --> 00:24:07,660
built remittance product that links with existing accounts.

483
00:24:08,140 --> 00:24:12,220
So it's funny. You've mentioned opera, finance and yellow card as competitors.

484
00:24:12,220 --> 00:24:16,740
But it's interesting that opera actually uses yellow card and finance for on and off.

485
00:24:17,660 --> 00:24:18,740
I meant a group, though.

486
00:24:18,740 --> 00:24:20,500
I shouldn't say I don't think they're competitors.

487
00:24:20,500 --> 00:24:22,340
I think they have parallels.

488
00:24:22,340 --> 00:24:25,940
They're all like bundled into one product for some of your audience

489
00:24:25,940 --> 00:24:29,300
that may not be familiar in some of the markets that we're talking about.

490
00:24:29,420 --> 00:24:31,260
When you show somebody a dollar,

491
00:24:31,260 --> 00:24:34,980
when you show them a dollar fiat currency, they don't know what that is.

492
00:24:35,340 --> 00:24:37,140
For them, dollar is USDT.

493
00:24:37,140 --> 00:24:40,940
Really, in Nigeria, there's some areas where they think of dollar as USDT.

494
00:24:41,180 --> 00:24:44,220
In these areas, do I think the transfer wise will come on top?

495
00:24:44,380 --> 00:24:45,700
Maybe not.

496
00:24:45,700 --> 00:24:47,940
Do I think products like opera will come on top?

497
00:24:47,940 --> 00:24:50,660
Absolutely. So it depends market to market.

498
00:24:50,940 --> 00:24:55,060
But I also think that this market is so big, there is space for everybody to play.

499
00:24:55,340 --> 00:24:58,580
It's also just a world in which a lot of this money movement.

500
00:24:58,580 --> 00:24:59,820
It's a bunch of trust.

501
00:24:59,820 --> 00:25:02,420
So opera specifically, I think they're well positioned

502
00:25:02,420 --> 00:25:06,180
because they have trust with the users from their browser product already.

503
00:25:06,300 --> 00:25:09,700
It's not a brand new branding going into the market and saying,

504
00:25:09,700 --> 00:25:12,460
hey, users for cross border remittance, people trust them.

505
00:25:12,820 --> 00:25:15,060
So I think we have a really good finding chance.

506
00:25:15,060 --> 00:25:17,140
But I also think that there's space for everybody to play.

507
00:25:17,180 --> 00:25:18,300
This is a big market.

508
00:25:18,300 --> 00:25:21,300
I think if transfer wise comes up tomorrow, and I don't know if they're already doing it,

509
00:25:21,300 --> 00:25:22,580
I'm sure they're looking at this already.

510
00:25:22,580 --> 00:25:25,460
But if transfer wise comes up tomorrow and says, hey, we'll do cross border remittance

511
00:25:25,460 --> 00:25:28,780
through stable coins, I think there's a world in which everybody plays in this market.

512
00:25:28,900 --> 00:25:30,660
Different markets, I think, will have different people,

513
00:25:30,660 --> 00:25:35,220
different products being taken up in Kenya, Nigeria and some of these markets.

514
00:25:35,220 --> 00:25:38,340
Are you seeing a lot of business USDT accounts?

515
00:25:38,820 --> 00:25:40,620
Yeah, it's not us.

516
00:25:40,620 --> 00:25:42,620
That's just so much already.

517
00:25:42,620 --> 00:25:44,420
Sub-Saharan Africa, I will club.

518
00:25:44,460 --> 00:25:50,100
Sub-Saharan Africa does so much cross border settlement with Asia and a lot.

519
00:25:50,140 --> 00:25:53,380
I want to say 70% of that settlement happens in USDT.

520
00:25:53,580 --> 00:25:54,940
If I don't have my facts right.

521
00:25:54,940 --> 00:25:57,420
And again, it's not official numbers that I'm getting.

522
00:25:57,700 --> 00:26:01,020
But just based on what I know, 70% of that settlement happens in USDT.

523
00:26:01,300 --> 00:26:02,660
So it's funny you say this.

524
00:26:02,660 --> 00:26:05,380
We had launched MiniPay as a retail product.

525
00:26:05,380 --> 00:26:08,700
And what we learned was some of these people that were settling B2B

526
00:26:08,700 --> 00:26:12,740
settlements used to come and park some of their USDT on MiniPay

527
00:26:12,740 --> 00:26:15,780
when it was just a product in the browser in Africa only,

528
00:26:15,940 --> 00:26:19,060
because the yield on USDT on MiniPay was really high.

529
00:26:19,500 --> 00:26:20,860
What was the yield?

530
00:26:20,860 --> 00:26:23,140
It was a ridiculous amount, just on board.

531
00:26:23,140 --> 00:26:27,860
And we were, again, for context, the average balance on MiniPay wallet

532
00:26:27,940 --> 00:26:31,100
in Africa is between $10 plus minus.

533
00:26:31,140 --> 00:26:32,740
So these balances aren't very high.

534
00:26:32,740 --> 00:26:37,740
So when you give them like a yield of hypothetically 25% per annum or more,

535
00:26:38,020 --> 00:26:40,860
that yield is not too much for the retail user.

536
00:26:41,140 --> 00:26:43,900
But when you look at it from a commercial perspective,

537
00:26:43,900 --> 00:26:47,860
if you're getting 25% on a dollar denominated stablecoin, that's a lot.

538
00:26:47,900 --> 00:26:50,100
Nobody's giving you 25% yield on the dollar.

539
00:26:50,100 --> 00:26:52,500
And so we saw a bunch of people park their money,

540
00:26:52,740 --> 00:26:55,940
like the B2B settlers and cross-border payments guys,

541
00:26:56,020 --> 00:26:58,220
some of their money into MiniPay accounts.

542
00:26:58,220 --> 00:27:00,420
And then we were like, oh, we're going to have cap

543
00:27:00,420 --> 00:27:03,460
upper limits to balances for this yield activity.

544
00:27:03,780 --> 00:27:06,820
But it was great to see people found it very organically.

545
00:27:07,380 --> 00:27:09,980
And are you seeing financial products come on chain

546
00:27:09,980 --> 00:27:11,540
in those local economies?

547
00:27:11,980 --> 00:27:13,780
Yeah. So I'll give you an example.

548
00:27:13,780 --> 00:27:17,500
We have a protocol in the ecosystem called Heraka that does.

549
00:27:17,740 --> 00:27:21,500
So, again, I'm a really big proponent of meet users where they're at

550
00:27:21,500 --> 00:27:24,940
and not give them like, hey, this is a solution we've come up with.

551
00:27:25,140 --> 00:27:27,620
You don't have to fit your behavior to this solution.

552
00:27:27,700 --> 00:27:29,860
And so in these regions, for instance,

553
00:27:29,860 --> 00:27:33,020
sacos or savings and lending groups are very popular.

554
00:27:33,180 --> 00:27:34,940
And they're called different things in India.

555
00:27:34,940 --> 00:27:36,140
They're called kitties.

556
00:27:36,140 --> 00:27:38,380
And in Kenya, they're called sacos.

557
00:27:38,380 --> 00:27:40,860
And these groups are very prevalent across emerging markets.

558
00:27:41,100 --> 00:27:43,420
And so we have a protocol in the ecosystem called Heraka,

559
00:27:43,420 --> 00:27:45,860
which does micro loans and then now starting savings.

560
00:27:45,860 --> 00:27:48,220
So essentially, it's just like a savings and lending group

561
00:27:48,220 --> 00:27:51,300
where you save and lend within a specific user group.

562
00:27:51,300 --> 00:27:54,940
So you, me and three of our friends form a group and we save

563
00:27:54,940 --> 00:27:56,900
and lend within each other.

564
00:27:56,900 --> 00:27:58,100
And that's how it's done.

565
00:27:58,100 --> 00:28:01,580
It's behavioral that's happened across generations in these regions.

566
00:28:01,620 --> 00:28:05,260
And so when you bring that behavior on chain, it becomes really easy.

567
00:28:05,300 --> 00:28:06,500
Then you can do settlement.

568
00:28:06,500 --> 00:28:08,300
You can do accounting. Everything is on chain.

569
00:28:08,540 --> 00:28:11,020
So Heraka, I think, is a really good example of that.

570
00:28:11,300 --> 00:28:13,900
We're seeing people do employee payments.

571
00:28:14,180 --> 00:28:18,780
There's a protocol called Franklin that is doing stablecoin payments for payroll.

572
00:28:18,980 --> 00:28:21,620
One of my favorite examples that just came online

573
00:28:21,620 --> 00:28:24,220
in the ecosystem is this protocol called Bando.

574
00:28:24,220 --> 00:28:27,500
And you can buy gift cards to places like Target, Chipotle,

575
00:28:27,500 --> 00:28:32,140
Starbucks in the US, but very specific to even the regions that we service.

576
00:28:32,140 --> 00:28:35,660
So in Kenya and South Africa, you can get a gift card to ShopRite,

577
00:28:35,660 --> 00:28:37,100
which is the target of that region.

578
00:28:37,140 --> 00:28:38,940
And they have similar things in South America.

579
00:28:38,940 --> 00:28:41,940
So really interesting products are coming on chain.

580
00:28:42,100 --> 00:28:44,460
Some of them financial, some of them non-financial.

581
00:28:44,620 --> 00:28:46,660
I'm going to give a shout out of one specific product

582
00:28:46,660 --> 00:28:49,340
that is non-financial that I'm very fond of in the ecosystem.

583
00:28:49,420 --> 00:28:51,420
It's a music streaming app called a Bando.

584
00:28:51,700 --> 00:28:53,620
So think of it like Spotify.

585
00:28:53,620 --> 00:28:58,220
The reason it's on MiniPay right now is because you can pay on a daily basis.

586
00:28:58,220 --> 00:28:59,820
You can take a daily subscription.

587
00:28:59,820 --> 00:29:02,500
And again, for you and me, this seems ridiculous.

588
00:29:02,500 --> 00:29:05,340
Like, why would I want a daily subscription to a music streaming app?

589
00:29:05,420 --> 00:29:07,420
But for some of the regions that we're talking about,

590
00:29:07,420 --> 00:29:10,300
the monthly music streaming subscription is really expensive.

591
00:29:10,380 --> 00:29:12,700
So there's some days you can afford it and some days you can't afford it.

592
00:29:12,940 --> 00:29:15,980
And so the fact that you can break it down to such minute levels

593
00:29:15,980 --> 00:29:19,140
and do it on chain with a little bit of a stable coin is really interesting.

594
00:29:19,140 --> 00:29:22,620
And in Bando, if you guys are listening, has excellent music.

595
00:29:22,620 --> 00:29:24,060
It's Afrobeat. I love it.

596
00:29:24,060 --> 00:29:27,060
It's what's playing in the background when I'm doing deep focused work.

597
00:29:27,260 --> 00:29:28,420
But really great.

598
00:29:28,420 --> 00:29:30,820
We're constantly thinking about on chain financial.

599
00:29:30,820 --> 00:29:33,460
But like when some of these use cases come online where you're like,

600
00:29:33,500 --> 00:29:36,180
hey, there's a music streaming app that I could pay with stable coins.

601
00:29:36,420 --> 00:29:37,460
That's cool.

602
00:29:37,460 --> 00:29:40,420
I want to ask you one more question as we close out here,

603
00:29:40,420 --> 00:29:42,820
which was given all this framing you've offered,

604
00:29:42,940 --> 00:29:46,980
I'm a major believer that where we'll see value in this on chain economy

605
00:29:46,980 --> 00:29:49,780
is in these real use cases, real assets.

606
00:29:50,180 --> 00:29:55,740
Do you think blockchain platforms, applications need to have native tokens

607
00:29:55,820 --> 00:29:58,140
or tokens for themselves?

608
00:29:58,140 --> 00:30:00,860
Do you think that we need to have these,

609
00:30:01,060 --> 00:30:04,900
I would call them internet money tokens that the industry has been known for

610
00:30:04,900 --> 00:30:05,900
for a long time?

611
00:30:05,900 --> 00:30:08,460
Or do you think eventually that fades into the background?

612
00:30:08,900 --> 00:30:10,020
Funny you call them internet money.

613
00:30:10,020 --> 00:30:12,620
My mom calls it magic bean money.

614
00:30:12,620 --> 00:30:15,100
So I don't know whether we should have them or not.

615
00:30:15,100 --> 00:30:19,260
I know that in my ecosystem, the seller token is there for decentralized

616
00:30:19,260 --> 00:30:22,540
governance, and I think that's really, really critical.

617
00:30:22,700 --> 00:30:26,060
I think one of the things that we're trying to do in some form or the other,

618
00:30:26,060 --> 00:30:30,100
and whether you explicitly say it or not, is rebuild

619
00:30:30,340 --> 00:30:33,220
how any organization works or how financial systems work.

620
00:30:33,540 --> 00:30:34,580
That's why we're all in Web3.

621
00:30:34,580 --> 00:30:36,340
We're trying to reimagine some of this.

622
00:30:36,340 --> 00:30:38,940
I think decentralized governance is one of those areas.

623
00:30:38,940 --> 00:30:41,180
But I'm really like, this is a really cool thing.

624
00:30:41,380 --> 00:30:44,180
I believe in collective intelligence.

625
00:30:44,180 --> 00:30:47,580
And so decentralized governance is a really good example of collective intelligence.

626
00:30:47,740 --> 00:30:51,020
So the seller token, honestly, one of the big primary reasons

627
00:30:51,020 --> 00:30:55,100
to have seller token is decentralized governance, where the ecosystem

628
00:30:55,100 --> 00:30:58,740
and everybody in the community decides what is the future for the chain.

629
00:30:58,860 --> 00:31:01,420
So, for instance, to give you a really good example,

630
00:31:01,420 --> 00:31:04,220
we went from a layer one blockchain to a layer two blockchain.

631
00:31:04,220 --> 00:31:06,300
We successfully migrated to a layer two blockchain.

632
00:31:06,300 --> 00:31:10,500
But every single decision for the infrastructure was done in public

633
00:31:10,500 --> 00:31:12,300
through decentralized governance.

634
00:31:12,300 --> 00:31:16,140
And so every stack put out massive forum proposals

635
00:31:16,260 --> 00:31:18,940
on the seller forum to say, hey, this is why you should pick us.

636
00:31:18,940 --> 00:31:22,740
So we had proposals from OBStack, from Arbitrum, from Polygon ZK,

637
00:31:22,740 --> 00:31:24,380
from Matterlabs and things like that.

638
00:31:24,380 --> 00:31:27,380
And there was such an incredible healthy discussion in the community around

639
00:31:27,420 --> 00:31:28,660
which stack we should pick.

640
00:31:28,660 --> 00:31:30,020
So it wasn't centralized decision.

641
00:31:30,020 --> 00:31:32,540
It wasn't seller foundation or C-Lab taking the decisions.

642
00:31:32,660 --> 00:31:33,780
It's the community came together.

643
00:31:33,780 --> 00:31:36,980
And I think some of the things that came up in some of those community decisions,

644
00:31:36,980 --> 00:31:39,700
I was like, oh, wow, we weren't even thinking about this.

645
00:31:39,900 --> 00:31:43,140
And so I think collective intelligence is really, really interesting.

646
00:31:43,380 --> 00:31:46,100
Also, what it did was we've heard from different sources.

647
00:31:46,140 --> 00:31:50,500
If you're picking a layer two stack, seller forum is like Wikipedia.

648
00:31:50,660 --> 00:31:53,020
People actually go into the best seller forum to look at, oh,

649
00:31:53,140 --> 00:31:55,700
when they're doing a stacking decision, they go into seller forum and look at,

650
00:31:55,700 --> 00:31:57,140
oh, these are the things that were discussed.

651
00:31:57,140 --> 00:31:58,540
This is what the answers were.

652
00:31:58,540 --> 00:32:01,500
So I think decentralized governance is a really good example

653
00:32:01,500 --> 00:32:04,540
of why you need native tokens or magic bean money.

654
00:32:04,820 --> 00:32:07,780
Do I think we need as many as we have right now?

655
00:32:08,020 --> 00:32:10,300
I think there will be a world of consolidation,

656
00:32:10,300 --> 00:32:13,020
but I don't know when that world will be. But it's interesting.

657
00:32:13,340 --> 00:32:14,820
We were talking about collective intelligence.

658
00:32:14,820 --> 00:32:17,780
I think one of the reasons we specifically have been successful

659
00:32:17,780 --> 00:32:20,900
as an ecosystem is because we have regional DAOs also.

660
00:32:20,900 --> 00:32:22,460
So we have a DAO in Africa.

661
00:32:22,460 --> 00:32:24,100
We have a Latam DAO.

662
00:32:24,100 --> 00:32:25,540
We have a Thailand DAO.

663
00:32:25,540 --> 00:32:26,780
We have a Philippines DAO.

664
00:32:26,780 --> 00:32:29,860
And because we think that every region should have a say

665
00:32:29,860 --> 00:32:32,500
in how we represent in that region.

666
00:32:32,900 --> 00:32:34,940
And these DAO communities have been incredible

667
00:32:34,940 --> 00:32:37,260
and also very active in our governance process.

668
00:32:37,420 --> 00:32:41,220
And so as we're thinking about how do we do a go-to-market strategy

669
00:32:41,220 --> 00:32:44,460
for that specific DAO, it's been really helpful to have the communities

670
00:32:44,460 --> 00:32:47,100
in there have a massive say in some of those decision-makings.

671
00:32:47,500 --> 00:32:49,940
I'm a big proponent, again, of decentralized governance.

672
00:32:49,980 --> 00:32:51,580
I know it has some flaws.

673
00:32:51,580 --> 00:32:53,860
We've all experienced some flaws at decentralized governance.

674
00:32:53,860 --> 00:32:56,060
But I think overall, if you're able to manage it,

675
00:32:56,060 --> 00:32:58,940
I think incredible concept and excited to see where it by balls

676
00:32:58,940 --> 00:33:00,380
in the next five years.

677
00:33:00,380 --> 00:33:03,100
I guess if you're going to have a digital financial system

678
00:33:03,100 --> 00:33:05,460
or monetary system, you should have some form of

679
00:33:05,460 --> 00:33:07,740
democratic monetary decision-making.

680
00:33:08,340 --> 00:33:10,900
A hundred percent. We're a hundred percent aligned on that.

681
00:33:11,340 --> 00:33:12,860
Well, Isha, thank you for coming on.

682
00:33:12,860 --> 00:33:14,980
Hope you come back to chat with us again soon.

683
